messages:    6%|5         | 34/600 [12:28<12:21,  1.31s/it]["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nSome students attending a small university with a well-known choir live off campus. From the fact that all music majors are members of the choir, a professor in the music department concluded that none of the students who live off campus is a music major.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, the professor's conclusion is properly drawn if which one of the following is assumed?\n# Options:\nA) None of the students who live off campus is a member of the choir.\nB) All students who are members of the choir are music majors.\nC) Some of the students who do not live off campus are not music majors.\nD) All students who live on campus are music majors.\n<think>.\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, let's break down the given context and the professor's conclusion step by step.We need to determine which assumption, if made, would allow the professor's conclusion to be properly drawn.\n\n1.**Context Recap:**.\n   - Some students attending a small university with a well-known choir live off campus.\n   - All music majors are members of the choir.\n\n2.**Professor's Conclusion:**.\n   - The professor concluded that none of the students who live off campus is a music major.\n\n3.**Analyzing the Conclusion:**.\n   - The professor's conclusion is that no off-campus students are music majors.\n   - To properly draw this conclusion, we need to ensure that all off-campus students are not part of the choir.\n\n4.**Assumptions Analysis:**.\n   - **Option A:** None of the students who live off campus is a member of the choir.\n     - If this assumption is true, then since all music majors are members of the choir, no off-campus students can be music majors.\n     - This directly supports the professor's conclusion.\nAll students who do not live on campus are not music majors.\n\n5.**Conclusion:**. Therefore, my final option is: \nThe correct option is:"]
