 goal.\n  2. That these efforts, if expended, will actually achieve the goal.\n\n### Example:\nConsider a leader who tries to convince their followers that their efforts are necessary and will achieve the goal. However, despite this convincing, the followers remain unconvinced that their efforts will actually lead to the goal's attainment. In this scenario, the leader has successfully convinced the followers of the necessity of their efforts, but the followers still do not follow the leader to the goal.\n\n### Why Option A is Correct:\n- The context does not state that leaders must always succeed in leading their followers to the goal.\n- It only specifies that followers will follow if they are convinced of both the necessity and the achievability of their efforts.\n- Therefore, it is possible for a leader to convince followers of the necessity of their efforts but fail to lead them to the goal due to the lack of conviction about the achievability of the efforts.\n\n### Example Scenario:\n- Leader A convinces followers that their efforts are necessary for the goal.\n- Followers remain unconvinced that their efforts will actually achieve the goal.\n- Despite the leader's convincing, followers do not follow to the goal.\n\nThis scenario aligns with the context and explains why option A is correct. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>That example really helps clarify why option A is correct. Thank you for the detailed explanation.\n</\uff5cUser\uff5c><\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad the example helped clarify the situation. If you have any more questions or need further explanations, feel free to ask. Understanding these logical relationships is crucial for solving such problems. If you have any other questions or need additional examples, go ahead and ask! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Could you provide an example to illustrate why option C is incorrect based on the context?\n</\uff5cUser\uff5c><\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's provide an example to illustrate why option C is 