is not supported by his experiences (never winning). However, it does not clearly show a pattern of seeking out information that supports his belief while ignoring contradictory information. Therefore, this option does not strongly indicate confirmation bias.\n\nD) Xiaodong often worried when he heard some so-called prophets conclude that he was going to be in a car accident. Eventually, after a brush with another vehicle, Xiaodong trusted the prophet more.\n- This option describes a situation where Xiaodong's belief (that he would have a car accident) was supported by a prophet's prediction, leading him to trust the prophet more. This is a clear example of confirmation bias because Xiaodong sought out information (the prophet's prediction) that supported his belief and ignored contradictory information (the fact that he did not have a car accident). This option fits the definition of confirmation bias well.\n\nAfter analyzing all the options, both B and D are strong examples of confirmation bias. However, option B is more straightforward and directly aligns with the given definition of confirmation bias, as it clearly shows the manager seeking out information that supports his belief while ignoring contradictory information.\n\nTherefore, the most likely option that exemplifies confirmation bias is B.\n\n</think>\n<answer>\nB\n</answer>",
