      "text": " <think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, we need to identify which option fits the definition of an analogical argument. According to the context provided, an analogical argument involves comparing two objects based on their shared attributes and then inferring that they share additional attributes. Let's analyze each option step by step:\n\nA) \"Tao Yuanming does not bow for five buckets of rice, Li Bai does not destroy eyebrows and bend over power. This shows that ambitious people are not proud of high officials and generous salaries.\"\n- This option does not fit the definition of an analogical argument. It presents a comparison between two historical figures (Tao Yuanming and Li Bai) based on their actions, but it does not infer additional attributes based on shared attributes. Instead, it makes a generalization about ambitious people.\n\nB) \"Both light and sound have the phenomena of linear propagation, refraction, and reflection, so light, like sound, also has fluctuation.\"\n- This option fits the definition of an analogical argument. It compares light and sound based on their shared attributes (linear propagation, refraction, and reflection) and infers that they share another attribute (fluctuation). This is a clear example of analogical reasoning.\n\nC) \"Compared with some people who are irresponsible in their work and afraid of heavy work, Comrade Bethune is extremely responsible for his work and extremely enthusiastic towards his comrades and the people, which shows that Comrade Bethune has a great spirit of being unselfish and dedicated to others.\"\n- This option does not fit the definition of an analogical argument. It presents a comparison between Comrade Bethune and others based on their work ethic and dedication, but it does not infer additional attributes based on shared attributes. Instead, it makes a generalization about Comrade Bethune's character.\n\nD) \"Silver and copper are both metals with electrical conductivity, but the resistivity of silver is lower, so silver is more suitable to be a conductive material than copper.\"\n- This option does not fit the definition of an analogical argument. It presents a comparison between silver and copper based on their electrical conductivity and resistivity, but it does not infer additional attributes based on shared attributes. Instead, it makes a conclusion about their suitability for conductive materials based on their properties.\n\nAfter analyzing all the options, the correct option that fits the definition of an analogical argument is B.\n</think>\n<answer>\nB\n</answer>",
