      "text": "D\n\n<think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, we need to carefully analyze the context and the question. The context provides information about China's control over rare earths, which has led to accusations of monopolization. The question asks us to identify which statement, if true, would most effectively refute these accusations. \n\nLet's examine each option:\n\nA) \"Rare earths are non-renewable and important strategic resources.\"\n- This statement highlights the significance of rare earths but does not address China's control over their production and export. It does not directly refute the accusations of monopolization.\n\nB) \"At present, on a per capita basis, China is already a country with relatively scarce rare earth resources.\"\n- This statement suggests that China has limited rare earth resources, but it does not provide any information about China's control over these resources or its export policies. It does not directly refute the accusations.\n\nC) \"Since the 1980s, rare earths have been exploited indiscriminately in some parts of China, causing serious waste of resources and great damage to the environment.\"\n- This statement points out environmental issues related to the exploitation of rare earths but does not address the accusations of monopolization. It does not directly refute the accusations.\n\nD) \"In 2009, China's rare earth reserves accounted for 36% of the national reserves, the United States accounted for 13%, Russia accounted for 19%, Australia accounted for 5.4%; China's production accounted for 97% of the world's production, and the other three countries were all zero.\"\n- This statement directly addresses China's control over rare earths. It shows that China has a significant share of both reserves and production, which is the core of the accusations of monopolization. This information directly counters the idea that China is monopolizing resources.\n\nGiven this analysis, option D is the most appropriate to refute the countries' accusations of China monopolizing rare earth resources.\n</think>\n<answer>\nD\n</answer>",
