      "text": " <think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, we need to identify which option most effectively weakens the conclusion that senior intellectuals at the two universities will be welcomed by the promotion of clean vegetables. The conclusion is based on the assumption that these intellectuals will appreciate the convenience and time-saving benefits of clean vegetables. We need to find an option that challenges this assumption in the strongest way possible. Let's analyze each option step by step:\n\nA) \"I feel that the price of vegetables is only slightly higher than those sold in the general market.\"\n- This option addresses the cost aspect but does not directly challenge the convenience and time-saving benefits of clean vegetables. It suggests that the price might be a minor concern, but it does not weaken the primary argument about the convenience.\n\nB) \"Most families in these two universities hire part-time workers to do all kinds of housework, and the remuneration paid to part-time workers is less than the increase in expenses for buying clean vegetables.\"\n- This option directly challenges the convenience and time-saving benefits of clean vegetables. It implies that even if clean vegetables are more expensive, the cost savings from hiring part-time workers might outweigh the benefits of having clean vegetables. This weakens the conclusion that the senior intellectuals will be welcomed by the promotion of clean vegetables.\n\nC) \"The variety of clean vegetables is less than the vegetables sold in the general market. I am afraid that it cannot satisfy the tastes of so many teachers in these two universities.\"\n- This option addresses the variety of clean vegetables but does not directly challenge the convenience and time-saving benefits. It suggests that the clean vegetables might not meet the taste preferences of the teachers, but it does not weaken the primary argument about the convenience.\n\nD) \"Buying clean vegetables is still a new thing for most of the teachers in these two universities, and I am afraid that there will be a process of adaptation.\"\n- This option acknowledges that the practice of buying clean vegetables is new but does not directly challenge the convenience and time-saving benefits. It suggests that there might be a learning curve, but it does not weaken the primary argument about the convenience.\n\nAfter analyzing all the options, the one that most effectively weakens the conclusion is option B, as it directly challenges the primary benefit (convenience and time-saving) of clean vegetables.\n</think>\n<answer>\nB\n</answer>",
