es on the relationship between campaign funding and judicial decisions.\n     - This option is not a valid criticism of the argument based on the given information.\n\n   - **Option D**: \"The argument does not give the proportion of campaign funders among all the parties involved.\"\n     - The context only provides the proportion of justices supporting the patrons in cases where the party involved was their own campaign sponsor. It does not give the overall proportion of campaign funders among all parties involved.\n     - This option points out a significant gap in the argument, as it does not provide a comprehensive view of the relationship between campaign funding and judicial decisions.\n\n4. **Conclusion**:\n   - Option D is the most appropriate because it highlights a crucial piece of information that is missing from the argument. Without knowing the proportion of campaign funders among all parties involved, it is difficult to draw a complete and accurate conclusion about the relationship between campaign funding and judicial decisions.\n</think>\n<answer>\nThe correct option is D.\n</answer>",
