ter.**\n- Number of students who preferred the new method: 70\n- Number of students who understood the material better: 60\n- 70 > 60\n- This statement is true.\n\n**B) More students understood the material better than those who preferred the new method.**\n- Number of students who understood the material better: 60\n- Number of students who preferred the new method: 70\n- 60 < 70\n- This statement is false.\n\n**C) More students preferred the old method than those who understood the material better.**\n- Number of students who preferred the old method: 30\n- Number of students who understood the material better: 60\n- 30 < 60\n- This statement is false.\n\n**D) More students understood the material better than those who preferred the old method.**\n- Number of students who understood the material better: 60\n- Number of students who preferred the old method: 30\n- 60 > 30\n- This statement is true.\n\n### Conclusion:\nBased on the analysis, the correct statements are A and D. However, since we need to choose only one option, the most accurate and directly supported statement is:\n\n</think>\n<answer>\nA\n</answer><|User|>Alright, let's try one more.\n# Context:\nA survey was conducted to determine the effectiveness of a new teaching method. The survey included 100 students, and 60 of them reported that they understood the material better after using the new method. However, 40 students reported that they did not understand the material better. The survey also included a question asking students if they would prefer the new method over the old method. 70 students preferred the new method, while 30 preferred the old method.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following statements is true?\n# Options:\nA) More students preferred the new method than those who understood the material better.\nB) More students understood the material better than those who preferred the new method.\nC) More students preferred the old method than those who understo